//RS /// D-SERDE2=A / I9: project a codec owner onto its canonical bundle identity.
//RS ///
//RS /// A codec instance is keyed by the OWNER TYPE — `cx.rust_type` for AOT, the JIT
//RS /// program's function table for the resident tier, and `serde_codec` for the TIR
//RS /// evaluator all spell it `Owner::encode` / `Owner::decode`. Every imported
//RS /// nominal in TIR is spelled by its canonical identity: `register_imported_struct_shapes`
//RS /// registers it that way, and the struct-literal head resolves `alias.Type` through
//RS /// `foreign_type_identity` (it ICEs rather than keep the alias spelling). A LOCAL
//RS /// struct's declared field type is the one place the source spelling survives —
//RS /// `struct ImportedEnvelope { badge: library.Badge }` stores `library.Badge`
//RS /// verbatim — so a field read reaches this node under a spelling no codec is
//RS /// keyed by, and the evaluator refuses it with E0956 while AOT emits fine.
//RS /// Resolve it here, through the one resolver that splits an alias off a qualified
//RS /// name, so one type demands exactly one codec in every tier. `cx.rust_type`
//RS /// renders both spellings to the same Rust path (`Context.rs` foreign-identity arm
//RS /// and dotted-alias arm), so AOT emit is unchanged.
//RS ///
//RS /// A bare name is left alone unless the declaring context has already recorded
//RS /// its canonical module identity. Imported generated codecs are lowered under
//RS /// that identity, while their checked method bodies still carry source-local
//RS /// field spellings (for example `Expr` inside `Dual`). Resolve that local
//RS /// spelling before emitting a codec demand; core codec owners have no local
//RS /// identity and therefore keep the existing builtin path.
//RS fn canonical_codec_owner(ty: &Type, cx: &Cx) -> Type {
//RS     let Type::Named(name) = ty else {
//RS         return ty.clone();
//RS     };
//RS     if let Some(identity) = cx.local_type_identities.get(name) {
//RS         return Type::Named(identity.clone());
//RS     }
//RS     if !name.contains('.') {
//RS         return ty.clone();
//RS     }
//RS     match cx.imported_type_metadata_name(name) {
//RS         Some(identity) if identity != *name => Type::Named(identity),
//RS         _ => ty.clone(),
//RS     }
//RS }
//RS 
//RS /// Return the nominal identity used by the checked method target rows. Entry
//RS /// locals keep their source leaf; imported and canonical receivers use the
//RS /// NameLedger identity already shared by field/type lowering. An imported
//RS /// nominal also has a leaf row in `local_type_identities`, so only a leaf the
//RS /// entry itself declares maps back: Core `URL`'s canonical receiver must keep
//RS /// its identity, or the call names a bare `URL::method` no module declares.
//RS fn canonical_method_target_owner(cx: &Cx, name: &str) -> String {
//RS     if cx.jit_local_call_prefix.is_none() {
